`INVDB` is the Vorbis floor 1 inverse decibel table, indexed by a floor value from 0 to 255.

Errors: a module text `why` holds the reason of the last failure; `fail(reason)` stores it and gives false; `error()` gives it. Module `src` is a byte list holding the file being decoded; `sb(at)` gives the byte at at as an int.

Ogg pages (`pages()` gives a bool). It fills module lists: `pkt` (byte list, every packet's bytes one after another), `pk_at` and `pk_len` (int lists, each packet's start in pkt and its length), `pk_gran` (int list, per packet the granule position of the page on which the packet ends when it is the last packet ending on that page, otherwise minus 1) and `pk_last` (bool list, true when the packet ends on the stream's last page). It clears them all first. Walk pages from offset 0: a page needs at least 27 bytes and "OggS" at its start, otherwise stop walking (a page that does not fit in the file also stops the walk). Byte 4 is the version and must be 0 (else stop). Byte 5 holds the flags (2 means first page, 4 last page). The granule is the 64-bit little-endian signed value at 6, the serial number the 32-bit little-endian value at 14, the segment count the byte at 26, followed by that many lacing values; the page body follows the lacing values and is as long as their sum. Only pages with the first page's serial number are used. Bytes of the current packet build up in a module byte list `cur`: each lacing value appends that many body bytes to cur, and a lacing value under 255 finishes the packet (append cur to pkt with its start and length, then empty cur). Remember the index of the last packet finished on each page; after the page, when the page finished at least one packet and its granule is not minus 1, store the granule as that packet's pk_gran. A page with the last-page flag ends the walk. After the walk mark pk_last for every packet finished on the last page used. It fails with "no Ogg pages" when no packet was found, and "too few Vorbis headers" when fewer than three packets were found.

Bit reader over one packet (module ints `bp` bit position from the packet start, `bend` the packet's length in bits, `pbase` the packet's start in pkt, module bool `eop`). `bstart(k, skip)` points them at packet k with bp at skip bytes times 8 and eop false. `peek32()` gives the next 32 bits lowest first as a non-negative int, taking bits past the packet end as zero: build it from the five bytes starting at byte bp shifted right by 3 (bytes past the packet read as 0), shift right by bp and 7, keep the low 32 bits. `bits(n)` for n from 0 to 32: when bp plus n is past bend, set eop, move bp to bend and give 0; otherwise give the low n bits of peek32 and move bp on by n. `ilog(x)` gives the number of bits needed for x (0 for 0 or less, else one more than the position of its top set bit). `unpack(x)` turns a 32-bit Vorbis float into a real: mantissa the low 21 bits, exponent bits 21 to 30, negative when bit 31 is set; the value is the signed mantissa times 2 to the power exponent minus 788.

Codebooks live in module lists indexed by book number: ints `cb_dim`, `cb_n` (entries), `cb_len` (offset of its first entry in the int list `clen`, code lengths with 0 for an unused entry), `cb_code` (offset in the int list `ccode`, each entry's codeword already bit-reversed so it matches the stream read lowest bit first), `cb_fast` (offset in the int list `cfast`, 1024 slots), `cb_long` and `cb_nlong` (offset and count in the int list `clong` of the entries longer than 10 bits) and `cb_vq` (offset in the f32 list `cvq` of entries times dim values, or minus 1 when the book has no lookup). `book(b)` reads one codebook from the setup packet and gives a bool: the 24-bit sync value must be 0x564342 ("bad codebook sync"); dim is 16 bits, entries 24 bits (dim and entries must be at least 1, "bad codebook size"). One bit says ordered. Unordered: one bit says sparse; then for each entry, when sparse one flag bit (0 means unused, length 0) and when used or not sparse the length is 5 bits plus 1. Ordered: the current length starts at 5 bits plus 1; while fewer than entries are filled, read a count of ilog(entries minus the filled count) bits, give the next count entries the current length (more than entries fails, "bad codebook lengths") and add 1 to the current length. Codewords: an int list `avail` of 33 zeros; the first entry with a nonzero length gets code 0 and avail at i is 1 shifted left by 32 minus i for every i from 1 to its length; each later used entry of length l takes z equal to l, steps z down while z is above 0 and avail at z is 0 (z reaching 0 fails, "bad codebook lengths"), takes res from avail at z and sets avail at z to 0, gives the entry the code res, and when z differs from l sets avail at y to res plus 1 shifted left by 32 minus y for each y from l down to z plus 1. The stored code is the low l bits of the 32-bit bit reversal of res. Fast table: an entry of length up to 10 with stored code c fills every cfast slot whose low l bits equal c with the entry plus 1; slots no short code fills hold 0; entries longer than 10 bits go to clong in entry order. Lookup: 4 bits of type; type 0 has none; types 1 and 2 read minimum unpack(32 bits), delta unpack(32 bits), value bits as 4 bits plus 1, one sequence bit, then lookup-values multiplicands of value-bits bits each, where lookup values for type 1 is the largest r whose dim-th power is at most entries (start from the floor of entries to the power 1 over dim, then step up while r plus 1 still fits and down while r is too big) and for type 2 entries times dim; any other type fails ("bad lookup type"). The vq table holds for every entry e and k below dim the f32 value multiplicand times delta plus minimum plus last, where the multiplicand index is, for type 1, e divided by r to the power k modulo r, and for type 2 e times dim plus k; last starts at 0 for each entry and becomes the value after each k when the sequence bit is set; the arithmetic is in f32. Unused entries also get vq values. When eop was set during reading it fails ("setup header ends early").

`decode_entry(b)` reads one entry with book b and gives it, or minus 1 at the end of the packet or when no code matches: v is peek32; f is cfast at the book's fast offset plus the low 10 bits of v; when f is above 0 the entry is f minus 1, its length l, and when bp plus l is past bend set eop and give minus 1, otherwise move bp on by l and give the entry. Otherwise check each long entry in order: when the low l bits of v equal its stored code, do the same end check, move on by l and give it. No match sets eop and gives minus 1.

Floors live in module lists indexed by floor number: ints `fl_np` (partition count), `fl_pc` (offset of its partition classes in the int list `fpc`), `fl_mult` (multiplier 1 to 4), `fl_x` (offset of its X values in the int list `fx`; the int lists `fsort`, `flo` and `fhi` use the same offsets) and `fl_nx` (number of X values). Per floor f and class c below 16, the int lists `fcdim`, `fcsub` and `fcmaster` hold the class at f times 16 plus c, and `fcbook` holds subclass book j at (f times 16 plus c) times 8 plus j, minus 1 meaning none; they grow to the needed size when a floor is read. `floor(f)` reads one floor from the setup packet and gives a bool: the type is 16 bits; type 0 fails ("floor type 0 is not supported") and any type other than 1 fails ("bad floor type"). Type 1: partitions 5 bits, then each partition's class 4 bits; for each class up to the largest class used: dimensions 3 bits plus 1, subclasses 2 bits, a master book of 8 bits when subclasses is above 0, then for each of 2 to the power subclasses a book of 8 bits minus 1. Multiplier 2 bits plus 1, range bits 4 bits. X values: 0, then 1 shifted left by range bits, then for each partition, its class's dimensions values of range-bits bits each. More than 65 X values fails ("floor has too many points"); two equal X values fail ("bad floor points"). Book numbers must be below the codebook count ("bad book number"). fsort holds the indexes of the X values ordered by X ascending. For each i from 2, flo at i is the index j below i with the largest X value under X at i, and fhi at i is the index j below i with the smallest X value above X at i.

`floor_read(f, ch)` reads channel ch's floor 1 values for the current packet into the int list `fy` at ch times 72 plus i, and sets the bool list `fused` at ch. One bit 0 means unused (false). The range is 256, 128, 86 or 64 for multiplier 1 to 4. fy at 0 and 1 are each ilog(range minus 1) bits. Then for each partition with class c: cbits is the class's subclasses, csub is 1 shifted left by cbits minus 1, cval is decode_entry of the master book when cbits is above 0 (else 0); for each of the class's dimensions the book is fcbook at the low cbits of cval (cbits low bits via csub), cval shifts right by cbits, and the next fy value is decode_entry of that book when the book is not minus 1, else 0. When eop was set during the floor, the channel is unused.

`render_point(x0, y0, x1, y1, x)` gives y0 plus (or minus when y1 is below y0) the integer part of the absolute difference of y1 and y0 times (x minus x0) divided by (x1 minus x0). `render_line(x0, y0, x1, y1, out, at, n)` writes INVDB values into the f32 list out at at plus x for x from x0 below x1 and below n: with dy the difference y1 minus y0, adx x1 minus x0, ady the absolute dy, base dy divided by adx truncated toward zero, sy base minus 1 when dy is negative else base plus 1, ady reduced by the absolute base times adx, y starting at y0 and err at 0: write y at x0, then for each later x add ady to err and when err reaches adx take adx off err and add sy to y, otherwise add base to y, and write y. Written values clamp y to 0 to 255 before the lookup.

`floor_curve(f, ch, n2, out, at)` builds channel ch's floor curve of n2 values into out at at. With range as above: step flags start false except at 0 and 1; final values at 0 and 1 are fy at 0 and 1. For each i from 2: low flo at i, high fhi at i, predicted render_point of the X and final values of low and high at X at i; val is fy at i, highroom is range minus predicted, lowroom is predicted, room is twice the smaller of the two (highroom when highroom is below lowroom, else lowroom). When val is not 0: mark low, high and i, and the final value is, when val is at least room, val minus lowroom plus predicted when highroom is above lowroom, else predicted minus val plus highroom minus 1; when val is below room, predicted minus (val plus 1) halved when val is odd, else predicted plus val halved. When val is 0 the final value is predicted and i stays unmarked. Then draw: lx is 0, ly the final value at the first sorted index times the multiplier, hx 0 and hy 0; for each sorted position from 1 whose index is marked: hy is its final value times the multiplier, hx its X; render_line from lx, ly to hx, hy; lx and ly become hx and hy. When hx is below n2, fill from hx to n2 with render_line from hx, hy to n2, hy.

Residues live in module int lists indexed by residue number: `rs_type`, `rs_begin`, `rs_end`, `rs_psize`, `rs_ncls` and `rs_book` (the classbook), and the int list `rs_books` holding residue r's book for class c and pass j at (r times 64 plus c) times 8 plus j, minus 1 meaning none. `residue(r)` reads one: type 16 bits (above 2 fails, "bad residue type"), begin 24 bits, end 24 bits, partition size 24 bits plus 1, classifications 6 bits plus 1, classbook 8 bits; then per class a cascade of 3 low bits plus, when the next bit is set, 5 more bits times 8; then per class and pass j below 8, a book of 8 bits when cascade bit j is set, else minus 1. Book numbers must be below the codebook count ("bad book number").

Mappings live in module int lists indexed by mapping number: `mp_sub` (submap count), `mp_ncp` (coupling steps), `mp_cp` (offset in the int lists `cmag` and `cang`), with `mux` holding the submap of channel ch at mapping times 255 plus ch, and `smfloor` and `smres` holding submap s's floor and residue at mapping times 16 plus s. `mapping(m)` reads one: type 16 bits must be 0 ("bad mapping type"); one bit then 4 bits plus 1 gives the submap count, else 1; one bit then 8 bits plus 1 gives the coupling steps, else 0, each reading a magnitude and an angle channel of ilog(channels minus 1) bits (equal channels or channels not below the channel count fail, "bad channel coupling"); 2 reserved bits must be 0; with more than one submap each channel reads a 4-bit mux (not below the submap count fails), else all 0; then each submap skips 8 bits and reads a floor and a residue of 8 bits each (not below their counts fails).

Modes: module int lists `md_block` (block flag) and `md_map` (mapping); `mode(i)` reads a block flag of 1 bit, window type 16 bits and transform type 16 bits (both must be 0, "bad mode"), and a mapping of 8 bits (not below the mapping count fails).

`headers()` gives a bool after pages(). Packet 0 must be at least 30 bytes with byte 0 equal to 1 and "vorbis" at 1 ("not a Vorbis stream"); version is the 32-bit little-endian value at 7 and must be 0; module int `chans` is the byte at 11 (0 fails); module int `rate` is the 32-bit little-endian value at 12 (0 fails); the byte at 28 gives module ints `bs0` as 1 shifted left by its low 4 bits and `bs1` by its high 4 bits (each from 64 to 8192 and bs0 at most bs1, else "bad block sizes"); bit 0 of the byte at 29 must be 1 ("bad identification header"). Packet 1 must start with 3 and "vorbis" ("bad comment header"). Packet 2 must start with 5 and "vorbis" ("bad setup header"); read it from byte 7: codebook count 8 bits plus 1 and each book; time count 6 bits plus 1, each a 16-bit value that must be 0; floor count 6 bits plus 1 and each floor; residue count 6 bits plus 1 and each residue; mapping count 6 bits plus 1 and each mapping; mode count 6 bits plus 1 and each mode (module int `nmodes`); a final bit must be 1 ("bad setup header"). It clears every setup list first.

Windows: `slope(n)` gives an f32 list of n halved values, value i being the sine of half pi times the square of the sine of half pi times (i plus 0.5) divided by n halved, worked out in reals. Module f32 lists `win0` and `win1` hold slope(bs0) and slope(bs1).

Inverse MDCT. Twiddle tables are made once per block size, never inside a transform: `tables(n)` (n a block size; it does nothing when module int `tab_n0` or `tab_n1` already equals n, filling slot 0 the first time and slot 1 the next) stores, for h equal to n divided by 4: the f32 lists `fc` and `fs` (slot 0 in `fc0`/`fs0`, slot 1 in `fc1`/`fs1`) of h halved values, value k being the cosine and minus the sine of 2 pi k over h; and the f32 lists `wc` and `ws` (slots `wc0`/`ws0` and `wc1`/`ws1`) of h values, value k being the cosine and sine of pi over (n halved) times (k plus 1/8); all worked out in reals and stored as f32. `fft(re, im, n, c, s)` transforms the f32 lists re and im (the first n entries, n a power of two) in place with the forward complex FFT (each output k is the sum over j of input j times e to the minus 2 pi i j k over n): bit-reversal permutation, then radix-2 butterflies; for a span sp the twiddle k below sp halved is c and s at k times (n divided by sp), read from the f32 lists c and s (the block size's fc and fs). No cos or sin is called inside fft. `imdct(x, xat, n, out)` takes n halved coefficients M from the f32 list x at xat and writes n values to the f32 list out (resized to at least n): it calls tables(n) and uses that slot's lists; h is M halved and q is M divided by 4; the twiddle w at k for k below h has cosine wc at k and sine ws at k (no cos or sin call); with ev the coefficient at 2k and od minus the coefficient at M minus 1 minus 2k, the FFT input k is real od times sin w minus ev times cos w, imaginary od times cos w plus ev times sin w; run fft of size h with the slot's fc and fs; then for each k below h, val is w at k times the conjugate of output k (complex product); out is cut into four quarters of h values each; for k below q with fi 2k and ri h minus 1 minus 2k: quarter 0 at ri is minus the imaginary part of val, quarter 1 at fi the imaginary part, quarter 2 at ri the real part, quarter 3 at fi the real part; for k from q with fi 2 times (k minus q) and ri h minus 1 minus fi: quarter 0 at fi is minus the real part, quarter 1 at ri the real part, quarter 2 at fi the imaginary part, quarter 3 at ri the imaginary part. `imdct_into(x, out)` is the public form: n is twice x's length, it calls imdct from offset 0.

`residue_read(r, chs, n2)` decodes residue r for the channels listed in the int list chs (each with a bool in the bool list `dnd`, do not decode) into the f32 list `rv` (channel ch's n2 values at ch times bs1 halved; already zero). Type 2: when every listed channel is marked do not decode, do nothing; otherwise decode one vector of chs length times n2 values into the f32 list `rtmp` (zeroed) as format 2 and then hand value i to listed channel i modulo the count at position i divided by the count. Types 0 and 1 decode each listed channel's own vector of n2 values, skipping marked channels. The decode: size is n2 (times the channel count for type 2); begin and end are rs_begin and rs_end limited to size; psize the partition size; cw the classbook's dim; partitions (end minus begin) divided by psize; nothing to read when that is 0. Classifications go in the int list `rcls` at vector index times (partitions plus cw) plus partition. For pass 0 to 7: p starts at 0; while p is below partitions: in pass 0, for each vector read temp with decode_entry of the classbook (minus 1 stops the whole residue) and for i from cw minus 1 down to 0 store temp modulo the class count at partition i plus p, then divide temp by the class count; then for i below cw while p is below partitions: for each vector, its class at p gives book rs_books at that class and pass, and when the book is not minus 1 decode the partition at begin plus p times psize; p goes up by 1. A partition with book b, of dim d: format 0 reads psize divided by d vq entries, entry j adding its value k to the vector at offset plus j plus k times (psize divided by d); formats 1 and 2 read entries one after another, each adding its d values to the next d places. A decode_entry of minus 1, or a book with no vq table, stops the whole residue (values decoded so far stay).

`packet(k, out)` decodes audio packet k and appends its frames to the f32 list out interleaved by channel; it gives the frame count appended. An empty packet, or a first bit of 1, gives 0 and changes nothing. Mode is ilog(nmodes minus 1) bits (not below nmodes gives 0); blockflag md_block of the mode; n is bs1 when it is 1, else bs0; a long block skips two window bits; n2 is n halved; the mapping is md_map of the mode. Per channel ch with submap mux at ch, floor_read with the submap's floor; dnd at ch is true when the floor is unused; when used, floor_curve into the f32 list `flr` at ch times bs1 halved, otherwise fill those n2 values with 0. For each coupling step when exactly one of its magnitude and angle channels is marked, unmark both. Zero rv's first n2 values of every channel. For each submap s collect the channels whose mux is s and run residue_read with its residue. Then undo coupling for steps from the last down to the first: for each i below n2 with m the magnitude value and a the angle value: when m is above 0, a above 0 gives magnitude m and angle m minus a, otherwise magnitude m plus a and angle m; when m is not above 0, a above 0 gives magnitude m and angle m plus a, otherwise magnitude m minus a and angle m. Then for each channel not marked, multiply flr by rv for i below n2. For each channel run imdct of flr at ch times bs1 halved with size n into a module f32 list `ibuf`. Overlap (module int `prev` the previous block size, 0 before the first audio packet; module f32 list `ovl` holding each channel's right half of the last block at ch times bs1 halved): when prev is 0, nothing is output. Otherwise the frame count is prev divided by 4 plus n divided by 4, worked out per channel into frames: with equal sizes, frame i below n2 is ovl at i times the slope (win1 when both are long, else win0) at n2 minus 1 minus i plus ibuf at i times the slope at i; a long block then a short one, start (bs1 minus bs0) divided by 4: frames below start copy ovl, then for i below bs0 halved, frame start plus i is ovl at start plus i times win0 at bs0 halved minus 1 minus i plus ibuf at i times win0 at i; a short block then a long one: for i below bs0 halved, frame i is ovl at i times win0 at bs0 halved minus 1 minus i plus ibuf at start plus i times win0 at i, then frames from bs0 halved copy ibuf from start plus bs0 halved up to bs1 halved. Clamp every frame value to minus 1 to 1. Save ibuf's values from n2 to n as the channel's ovl, set prev to n. Output grows once per packet: resize out by the frame count times the channel count, then write frame i of channel ch at the old length plus i times the channel count plus ch (never push sample by sample); clamp with plain comparisons.

`decode(data, pcm)` gives channels, rate and a bool: empty pcm, copy data to src, run pages and headers (any failure gives 0, 0, false), set prev to 0, reset tab_n0 and tab_n1 to 0, build the windows, then decode audio packets from 3 on into pcm, keeping a frame total. Trimming: at the first audio packet whose pk_gran is not minus 1, with s the total so far: when the packet is not on the last page and s is above the granule, the start skip is s minus the granule and the first granule is 0; otherwise the first granule is the granule minus s when that is above 0, else 0. After all packets, when the last packet with a pk_gran has granule g, the frame count is limited to g minus the first granule (when that is at least 0); drop the start skip frames from the front and the frames past the limit from the end. A stream with no audio frames still succeeds. `is_vorbis(b)` gives whether b has at least 35 bytes, "OggS" at 0 and the byte 1 then "vorbis" at 28. `error()` gives the reason of the last failure.
